  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic policy reform and management and private sector development</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia, through the Australian Infrastructure Financing Facility for the Pacific (AIFFP), is providing AUD72 million of concessional, results-based financing to the Government of Fiji.  The financing is supporting the renewal or resealing of more than 1.5 million square metres of road surface throughout Fiji, as well as replacement of nine bridges critical to the economy.  Climate data is integrated into all the bridge designs, helping to improve resilience to shocks and stresses, and providing up to a 100-year design life.  All road renewal contractors are locally registered and employ a majority of local Fijian workers, supporting local content.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Infrastructure</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The AIFFP employed locally registered contractors as a part of its loan and grant package for the &#x2018;Fiji Transport Infrastructure Restoration Project&#x2019;, while supporting the FRA&#x2019;s capacity to improve safety practices in road maintenance services. In 2023, the AIFFP piloted and refined a training accreditation program in collaboration with the Australia Pacific Training Coalition (APTC), that customised the Australian-accredited General Construction Induction Card (WHS &#x2013; Civil Works Qualification WHSCW1001). In June 2024, the FRA&#x2019;s electrical inspectors and personnel from the road renewal contracts received training, which included the delivery of International Skills Training (IST) certification to 3 of FRA&#x2019;s safety officers, to ensure the training can continue to be delivered in-house over the long term. The FRA is now the first organisation in Fiji to require its inspectors and roads maintenance contractors to hold an industry recognised WHS certification, over and above minimum WHS requirement</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
